---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Aside from "../components/Aside.astro";
import { url } from "../lib/site";
const sample =
  "# A little room to think\n\nToday, I want to **make something useful**.\n\n- [x] Open a fresh page\n- [ ] Write a first thought\n\n## A useful link\n\n[Markdown guide](https://www.markdownguide.org/)\n\n| Idea | Next step |\n| --- | --- |\n| A better README | Write the introduction |";
---

<Base
  title="Getting Started with Paperling: A Markdown Guide"
  description="Open your first Markdown file in Paperling, try live preview, save a note, and explore math, diagrams, and optional AI at your own pace."
  path="guide/"
  crumbs={[{ label: "Getting started", path: "guide/" }]}
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="page-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">FIVE MINUTES. ONE FRESH PAGE.</p>
      <h1>
        From opening a file
        <br />
        to <em>finding your flow.</em>
      </h1>
      <p>
        You don't need a system before you can write a note. Start with one file
        and let the rest follow.
      </p>
    </header>
    <div class="article-layout">
      <article class="prose">
        <h2>1. Open a note</h2>
        <p>
          <a href={url("download/")}>Download Paperling</a> for your device and
          launch the app. Open an existing .md file, or create a new document.
          On desktop, Ctrl+O opens a file and Ctrl+N creates a note; use Cmd
          instead of Ctrl on macOS.
        </p>
        <h2>2. Make a little structure</h2>
        <p>
          Markdown is plain text with a few conventions. A # starts a heading,
          pairs of asterisks make text bold, and a dash starts a list. Try this
          in Code view:
        </p>
        <pre><code>{sample}</code></pre>
        <h2>3. Find your favorite view</h2>
        <p>
          Use Reader view to read the rendered document. Code view gives you the
          source. Split view keeps both alongside each other. On desktop, Ctrl+E
          toggles Reader/Code and Ctrl+backslash toggles Split; use Cmd on
          macOS.
        </p>
        <h2>4. Give it a home</h2>
        <p>
          Save your document with a .md extension in a folder you control.
          Ctrl+S saves; Ctrl+Shift+S uses Save As (Cmd on macOS). Keep backups
          of important work. Optional auto-save can be configured in settings.
        </p>
        <h2>5. Add only what you need</h2>
        <p>
          Try the{" "}
          <a href={url("features/math-and-diagrams/")}>
            math and diagram syntax
          </a>{" "}
          for technical notes,{" "}
          <a href={url("features/focused-writing/")}>focus mode</a> for a longer
          draft, or <a href={url("features/ai-assistant/")}>optional AI</a> when
          you want another perspective.
        </p>
        <h2>Taking your document elsewhere</h2>
        <p>
          The original stays Markdown. The{" "}
          <a href={url("features/export/")}>Export menu</a> offers shareable
          formats. Check the exported document before sending it, especially
          when it includes local images, equations, or diagrams.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a href={url("shortcuts/")}>
            Keep the keyboard shortcut reference handy
          </a>{" "}
          while you settle in.
        </p>
      </article>
      <Aside />
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
